

You are being tested on whether you understand the 3D double-ended helix / helix–anti-helix construction and its relationship to the HP pencil, rather than merely recognizing terminology.

Starting from no diagrams, reconstruct the construction.

1. Define the full carrier C=C_+\sqcup C_-, identifying what the helix and anti-helix represent and what data live on their fibers.
2. Define the single global involution J:C_+\leftrightarrow C_-. Explain why treating the two strands as possessing independent/local reflection symmetries changes the mathematics.
3. Follow one generic point/fiber through the complete dimensional reduction

> 3D\ {\rm carrier}\rightarrow 2D\ {\rm Cayley/Möbius\ projection}
> \rightarrow {\rm unit\ circle\ reflection}
> \rightarrow 1D\ {\rm logarithmic\ readout}.
>

At every arrow state:

* what information is retained,
* what appears to be discarded,
* how the ledger makes the projection reversible,
* and which involution acts after projection.

4. Prove or explicitly derive the intertwining condition

> \pi_{2D}\circ J=J_{\rm Cayley}\circ\pi_{2D}.
>

Do not simply assert that symmetry is preserved.

5. Show how the global helix/anti-helix exchange becomes the functional-equation reflection after logarithmic readout, and explain why the fixed locus becomes

> \Re s=\frac12.
>

Distinguish carefully between geometry producing the reflection structure and assuming anything about the location of zeros.

6. Now introduce the HP pencil from its actual definition. Give an explicit dictionary

> \boxed{\text{3D carrier object}}
> \longleftrightarrow
> \boxed{\text{HP object}}
>

for every correspondence you believe exists: strands, fibers, involution, projection, ledger, clock/readout, spectral parameter, eigenvalues/zeros, and any warp/control parameter.

Every missing correspondence must be marked UNKNOWN rather than inferred.

7. Identify the first mathematical statement at which the HP construction uses information supplied by the double-ended helix geometry that HP does not already possess internally. Write that statement as an equation, lemma, or falsifiable proposition.
8. Perform the reverse test: starting only with the HP pencil, state exactly what additional structure is required to reconstruct the 3D double-ended helix. If HP determines the carrier uniquely, prove it. If several carriers produce the same HP data, characterize that degeneracy.
9. Destruction test: replace the global J with two independently chosen strand involutions while keeping the projected HP data as unchanged as possible. Determine the first invariant or identity that fails. If nothing fails, then the claimed global geometry is not actually encoded by HP.
10. Finally classify the relationship as exactly one of:

A. HP is a coordinate/projection representation of the double-ended helix.
B. HP is a spectral shadow of the helix and loses recoverable information.
C. HP and the helix are equivalent after adjoining the ledger.
D. The helix supplies an additional constraint on otherwise more-general HP pencils.
E. No demonstrated equivalence currently exists.

Give the classification before discussing RH consequences.

Anti-cheating condition

You get no credit for saying that conjugation, the functional equation, or symmetry “obviously” identifies the constructions. The test is passed only if you produce the explicit maps and identify which diagrams commute.

